[{"id":"net-report-fdre","itemId":"netlist","role":"warmup","promptHtml":"Vivado built lesson 6.1's <code class=\"nobreak\">acc8</code>, an 8-bit register that adds <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code> to itself at each rising edge where <code class=\"nobreak\">en</code> is 1, for the Arty A7-100T. Below is the Primitives table of its Utilization report: one row for each kind of cell in the netlist, with how many the design uses.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the FDRE row: eight, one flip-flop for each bit of <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code>, as lesson 6.1's Figure 1 draws them. The LUT2 and CARRY4 rows are the adder, and IBUF, OBUF and BUFG are the pins and the clock.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/acc8/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":180,"excerpt":["+----------+------+---------------------+","| Ref Name | Used | Functional Category |","+----------+------+---------------------+","| IBUF     |   11 |                  IO |","| OBUF     |    8 |                  IO |","| LUT2     |    8 |                 LUT |","| FDRE     |    8 |        Flop & Latch |","| CARRY4   |    2 |          CarryLogic |","| BUFG     |    1 |               Clock |"],"ask":"How many FDRE flip-flops does acc8 use?","answer":"8","numeric":true},{"id":"net-predict-wrap","itemId":"netlist","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">acc4</code> is lesson 6.1's <code class=\"nobreak\">acc8</code> with 4-bit <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code>: at each rising edge where <code class=\"nobreak\">en</code> is 1, <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> adds <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code>, and <code class=\"nobreak\">clr</code> sets it to 0, winning over <code class=\"nobreak\">en</code>. <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code> is drawn in hexadecimal, and <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> is 0 in cycle 0. Set <code class=\"nobreak\">acc[3]</code>, the top bit of <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code>, for every cycle.","explanationHtml":"<p><code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> runs 0, 5, 11, 11, 4, 3, 9, 0. At the edge that starts cycle 3, <code class=\"nobreak\">en</code> is 0, so <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> keeps 11. Then 11 + 9 = 20 and 4 + 15 = 19 do not fit in four FDREs, so <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> keeps 20 − 16 = 4 and 19 − 16 = 3. <code class=\"nobreak\">acc[3]</code> is 1 while <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> is 8 or more: cycles 2, 3 and 6. At the edge that starts cycle 7, <code class=\"nobreak\">clr</code> sets it back to 0, winning over <code class=\"nobreak\">en</code>.</p>","type":"waveform","wave":{"id":"acc-wrap","source":"hdl/level-06/acc4/acc4_tb.sv","timescale":"1ps","period":10000,"periodText":"10 ns","unit":"cycle","cycles":8,"signals":[{"label":"clk","path":"acc4_tb.clk","width":1,"role":"clock","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[1,"1"],[1.5,"0"],[2,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3,"1"],[3.5,"0"],[4,"1"],[4.5,"0"],[5,"1"],[5.5,"0"],[6,"1"],[6.5,"0"],[7,"1"],[7.5,"0"]]},{"label":"clr","path":"acc4_tb.clr","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"1"],[0.5,"0"],[6.5,"1"],[7.5,"0"]]},{"label":"en","path":"acc4_tb.en","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[0.5,"1"],[2.5,"0"],[3.5,"1"]]},{"label":"din[3:0]","path":"acc4_tb.din","width":4,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0000"],[0.5,"0101"],[1.5,"0110"],[2.5,"0111"],[3.5,"1001"],[4.5,"1111"],[5.5,"0110"],[6.5,"0010"],[7.5,"0000"]]},{"label":"acc[3]","path":"acc4_tb.acc3","width":1,"role":"signal","changes":[[0,"0"],[2,"1"],[4,"0"],[6,"1"],[7,"0"]],"samples":["0","0","1","1","0","0","1","0"]}]},"answer":"acc[3]"},{"id":"net-find-bug","itemId":"netlist","role":"stretch","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">sum_reg_bug</code> should add <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> through a register: <code class=\"nobreak\">sum</code> should show <code>a + b</code> one cycle after <code class=\"nobreak\">a</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">b</code> were set. Verilator warns that <code class=\"nobreak\">total</code> is never used. Pick the line that is wrong.","explanationHtml":"<p><code>assign sum = a + b;</code> reads the inputs, so <code class=\"nobreak\">sum</code> follows them at once and nothing reads <code class=\"nobreak\">total</code>. Synthesis keeps only what reaches a port, so it removes <code class=\"nobreak\">total</code>'s nine flip-flops and the adder that fed them. Verilator's warning points at the line that declares <code class=\"nobreak\">total</code>, but declaring it is fine; the assign should read <code class=\"nobreak\">total</code>.</p>","type":"find-bug","file":"hdl/level-06/sum_reg_bug/sum_reg_bug.sv","firstLine":1,"lines":[[{"text":"// Adds a and b through a register: sum shows a + b one cycle after a and","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"// b were set.","color":"var(--code-token-comment)","bold":false}],[{"text":"module","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"sum_reg_bug","color":"var(--code-token-function)","bold":false},{"text":" (","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":"       clk,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"7","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] a,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"input","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"7","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] b,","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"output","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"8","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] sum","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":");","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"  logic","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" [","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"8","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":":","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"0","color":"var(--code-token-constant)","bold":false},{"text":"] total;","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"always_ff","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"@","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":"(","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"posedge","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" clk)","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"    total ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"<=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" a ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"+","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" b;","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[],[{"text":"  ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"assign","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true},{"text":" sum ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"=","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" a ","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false},{"text":"+","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":false},{"text":" b;","color":"var(--code-foreground)","bold":false}],[{"text":"endmodule","color":"var(--code-token-keyword)","bold":true}]],"bugLine":14,"fixLine":"assign sum = total;"},{"id":"net-choice-small","itemId":"netlist","role":"core","promptHtml":"Vivado built <code class=\"nobreak\">acc4</code>, lesson 6.1's Listing 1 with 4-bit <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code>. Its Primitives table lists four FDREs and five LUTs (LUT2, LUT4, LUT5 and LUT6 cells), and no CARRY4. What does that tell you?","explanationHtml":"<p>A LUT can work out any function of its inputs, sums included, and for a 4-bit sum Vivado chose LUTs alone; <code class=\"nobreak\">acc8</code>, the 8-bit version, uses LUT2s and two CARRY4s. Nothing was removed: <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code> reaches a port and depends on the sum. An FDRE only holds a bit, and the Primitives table lists every cell in the netlist, however small.</p>","type":"choice","options":["Synthesis built the 4-bit adder from LUTs alone.","Synthesis removed the adder, because nothing reads it.","The adder is inside the FDREs.","The report left the CARRY4 out, because it is too small to count."],"correct":0},{"id":"net-report-acc4","itemId":"netlist","role":"core","promptHtml":"<code class=\"nobreak\">acc4</code> is lesson 6.1's Listing 1 with 4-bit <code class=\"nobreak\">din</code> and <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code>. Below is the Primitives table of the Utilization report Vivado wrote for it.","explanationHtml":"<p>Read the FDRE row: 4, one flip-flop for each bit of <code class=\"nobreak\">acc</code>, as for <code class=\"nobreak\">acc8</code>. The other rows are not <code class=\"nobreak\">acc8</code>'s halved, though: four kinds of LUT and no CARRY4. For a 4-bit sum, Vivado chose to work it out in LUTs alone.</p>","type":"report","source":"vivado/reports/acc4/arty-a7-100t/utilization.rpt","firstLine":180,"excerpt":["+----------+------+---------------------+","| Ref Name | Used | Functional Category |","+----------+------+---------------------+","| IBUF     |    7 |                  IO |","| OBUF     |    4 |                  IO |","| FDRE     |    4 |        Flop & Latch |","| LUT4     |    2 |                 LUT |","| LUT6     |    1 |                 LUT |","| LUT5     |    1 |                 LUT |","| LUT2     |    1 |                 LUT |","| BUFG     |    1 |               Clock |","+----------+------+---------------------+"],"ask":"How many FDRE flip-flops does acc4 use?","answer":"4","numeric":true}]